        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          The Spacelab 1 (SL-1) mission was the first flight of the European-built Spacelab, the first flight of non-career, non-American astronauts, and at the time, the longest mission and largest crew ever to fly.  The crew included Commander John W. Young, Pilot Brewster H. Shaw, Mission Specialists Owen K. Garriott and Robert A. Parker, and Payload Specialists Byron K. Lichtenberg and Dr. Ulf Merbold. Following nearly a decade of planning and development, the Spacelab was to make its first flight on SL-1, and so demonstrate the abilities of a multi-purpose reusable scientific research laboratory designed to be carried in the payload bay of the Space Shuttle.  The objectives of the Spacelab 1 mission were to verify the Spacelab system capability, to obtain valuable scientific, applications and technology data from the U.S./European multi-disciplinary payload, and to demonstrate the broad capability of Spacelab for scientific research.<P>This first Spacelab mission flew 38 experiment facilities and some 70 investigations.  One of the facilities, the Materials Science Double Rack, was used by the crew to conduct 30 experiments for 44 investigators in the areas of crystal growth, fluid physics and metallurgy.  Another unique facility was provided by the European Space Agency (ESA) and called the First Spacelab Payload (FSLP).  All investigations on SL-1 fell into five science categories:  Astronomy and Solar Physics, Space Plasma Physics, Atmospheric Physics, Earth Observations, Life Sciences, and Materials Science. <P>Out of the 15 life sciences experiments, seven were U.S. investigations and eight were European.  The experiments covered topics from the study of the radiation environment to the measurement of central venous pressure in weightlessness.<P>There were four U.S. human life sciences investigations.   The Effects of Prolonged Weightlessness on the Humoral Immune Response of Humans studied the levels of antibodies in serum from the crew in order to determine whether sustained weightlessness has an inhibitory effect on the immune system's response.  Studying the immune system's response in microgravity is important, since any abnormal function may affect the health of crewmembers on prolonged space flights.  The second investigation, Erythrokinetics in Man, measured specific factors governing the production of red blood cells in the body, or erythrokinetics, which differ in microgravity.  The third U.S. experiment investigated the Hoffman reflex to investigate the changes that occur in reflexes of the vestibular system in the near-weightless environment.  The fourth set of experiments, called the Vestibular Experiments, also studied the adaptive changes that occur in the vestibular system during space flight and the effects that remain for periods after return to Earth.  Vestibular experiments are important not only for understanding and preventing space motion sickness, but also for a better understanding of the overall mechanisms of vestibular function. <P>In life sciences, more than 90 percent of the planned operations and objectives of SL-1 were accomplished.  Some of the new and exciting scientific findings resulting from the mission include new evidence on the mechanism of caloric nystagmus, a possible higher threshold for sensing linear acceleration by the otolith, some increased dependence on visual cues for orientation, an unexpectedly low central venous pressure, the strong effect of microgravity on lymphocyte proliferation and a persisting circadian rhythm in neurospora.<P>Additional life sciences experiments performed during the STS-9 mission were those classified as Detailed Supplementary Objectives (DSOs).  A DSO is a NASA-sponsored investigation performed by Space Shuttle crewmembers, who also serve as test subjects.  These studies are designed to require minimal crew time, power and stowage.  Biomedical DSOs focus on operational concerns, including space motion sickness, cardiovascular deconditioning, muscle loss, changes in coordination and balance strategies, radiation exposure, pharmacokinetics and changes in the body's biochemistry.<P>The Spacelab 1 mission not only proved the value of merging manned space flight and space science, but also demonstrated NASA's commitment to involve the scientist in manned science missions from design through operation.  The SL-1 mission also showed the effectiveness of scientist-to-spacecraft interaction, proved the operational approach of having scientists flying as Payload Specialists, showed that Spacelab is a valuable capability for space science and served as a harbinger of a strong future for Space Station and other international space activities.<END>
